WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.900
Even in nearly three hours of supernatural
horror, It: Chapter Two can't tie up every

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.140
loose end left hanging.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.260
But never fear, because we're here to help
you look back over everything that happened

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.140
in the sequel to identify its most compelling
unanswered questions.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.660
Warning: Spoilers are ahead.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.880
Richie's flashback to the arcade strongly
implies he's gay and still in the closet.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:26.960
We see him playing Street Fighter with another
boy, and when Richie suggests they hang out

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.750
more, the boy spots his cousin, bully Henry
Bowers, and goes on the offensive, claiming

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:32.880
that Richie was hitting on him.

00:00:33.089 --> 00:00:38.800
When Pennywise appears to Richie shortly afterward,
he teases him, saying he knows his "dirty

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:39.520
little secret."

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.800
In 2016, Richie takes the death of Eddie harder
than any of the other Losers, and toward the

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.989
end of the film, he returns to a bridge where
he carved their initials together into the

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.820
rail, suggesting that Richie's feelings for
Eddie went beyond friendship.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.540
We're never told for sure, but it seems likely.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.540
It's tough not to wonder exactly how the grown-up
Losers ever get in and out of Derry without

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.280
being arrested.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.500
Their reunion dinner ends with them trashing
their private dining room when Pennywise unleashes

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.320
his illusions.

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:12.299
When the hostess walks in, she finds Mike
smashing a chair into the table, yet no one

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.080
so much as scolds them for it.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:18.460
Richie screams at a little kid as they leave
the restaurant, and Bill is there to watch

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.100
that same little boy get devoured by It at
the carnival.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:26.820
Most egregious, Richie buries a hatchet in
Bowers' head in the Derry Library.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.700
Yet no police officers show up to so much
as question the Losers about anything.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.620
We never find out how they explain Eddie's
disappearance.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.740
When Stanley's widow sends them all a letter
he wrote before killing himself, there aren't

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.960
any follow-up calls from curious detectives.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.320
The only police you're likely to notice in
the film are the ones beating the hell out

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.460
of the young Henry Bowers in a flashback.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.180
It's like the cops just disappeared.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:50.600
"What is going on here?"

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.560
"What, are they just screwin' with us?"

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.560
"Where are the cops?"

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.960
It's not just the police who are missing.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.859
Obviously people live in Derry, but judging
by the film, outside of a few scenes at the

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:01.420
carnival, it's hard to tell.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.210
The bed and breakfast where most of the Losers
are staying is all but deserted.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.379
They're constantly yelling, arguing, and banging
things around in the entryway.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.140
Eddie is attacked in his room by Henry, who
stabs him in the mouth.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.140
Eddie stabs Bowers in the chest, and the lunatic
retreats out the window.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.600
No one ever comes around to ask about the
blood or the broken furniture.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:25.700
When Bill grabs the same little boy Richie
yelled at earlier in broad daylight and yells

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.950
at him that he has to get out of town, there's
no one around to ask him what he thinks he's

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:30.500
doing.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:35.140
When the Losers emerge from It's lair and
the house collapses into the earth, there's

00:02:35.159 --> 00:02:36.620
no one around then, either.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.120
The streets are even deserted when they return
to downtown.

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.940
Derry seems like a ghost town.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:46.220
In Stepen King's novel, the writer makes it
clear that as threatening as It is, the monster

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:50.060
is not the only powerful force with a stake
in what happens in Derry.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:55.480
Before their battle with It, the young Losers
encounter a godlike space turtle named Maturin,

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.659
a benevolent spiritual force.

00:02:57.659 --> 00:03:01.799
While the direct help the turtle can offer
the heroes is minimal, it does remind them

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.510
that they are not alone in their battle.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.260
But the turtle exists in little more than
Easter eggs in the It films.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.360
The young Bill finds a LEGO turtle in Georgie's
room in 2017's It; in the sequel, there's

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.280
a prominent turtle model in a classroom.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.780
Those seem to be the only direct nods to Maturin.

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:18.860
"Where are the turtles?

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.660
Where are the turtles?"

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:20.380
"Come on, guys.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.390
Get outta here."

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.040
"Where are the turtles!?"

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.319
We know It crashed to Earth a long time ago,
and that its existence was well known to the

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.540
Native Americans who once lived where Derry,
Maine is now.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.340
It's made of Deadlights.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.440
That's about all we know.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.780
In his final battle with the Losers, Pennywise
refers to himself as the "eater of worlds."

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.120
Is it just bragging or is there more to It
than we know?

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:49.200
This is another example of more being revealed
in King's books, particularly his Dark Tower

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.680
series, but even there we don't get a complete
picture.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.320
Not to mention the fact that there's no reason
the It of the films and the It of the books

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.380
has to be the same entity.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.640
When the Losers reunite, we learn that with
the exception of Mike, they've mostly forgotten

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:03.860
everything about Derry.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:08.640
But over time, we discover at least one of
them maybe hadn't forgotten all, nor kept

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.720
their attentions away from the town entirely.

00:04:10.989 --> 00:04:15.879
Beverly is surprised to learn about her father's
death when Mrs. Kersh informs her of it, but

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:20.329
Eddie remembers all the details of his mother's
passing, volunteering the details of her cancer

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.100
to the pharmacist, Mr. Keene.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.740
That could mean a couple things: one, that
Eddie at the very least kept up with his mother

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:30.870
in Derry until her death, giving him some
lingering connection to the town.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.040
Or two, that she also left the town at some
point.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.040
We don't find out which is the case.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.140
Though there are some brief mentions in later
King novels including The Tommyknockers and

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:45.830
the Dark Tower series, the Losers' final battle
with Pennywise seems to have been the end

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:50.180
for the monster, at least as far as It's influence
in Derry was concerned.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.740
A King fan would likely argue that a return
of Pennywise in the movies would be sacrilege.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:00.150
But if It: Chapter Two rakes in the kind of
money it's expected to, Hollywood might be

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.690
fine with a little sacrilege.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.600
"Bitter, because people die."

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.100
"Right."

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.000
"And sweet, because you're making money."

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:09.500
"Exactly."

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:14.560
The questions It: Chapter Two leaves unanswered
could lay a narrative foundation for the villain's

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:15.060
return.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.180
If It was an alien, couldn't there be more
like It?

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.360
And what if It laid eggs?

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.960
In the book, the Losers indeed have to destroy
a clutch of eggs It laid.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:28.280
Could a sequel focus on some baby Its?

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.860
Time and ticket sales will tell.

